From ec560c263345b2ffa7b52631afeb5c6bab9d33e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wray-lee <wray-lee@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 07:09:35 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] feat(slack): show the task lifecycle as reactions

Slack had no reaction support at all: `status_reactions` and
`reaction_emojis` existed only on Telegram, and the lifecycle hooks that
drive them live in the Telegram polling loop, which Slack never runs.

Add the same configuration to `channels.slack.accounts.<id>` and drive it
from the bus path instead:

- `config_types.SlackReactionEmojisConfig` holds Slack reaction *names*
  (`eyes`, `hourglass_flowing_sand`, `white_check_mark`, `x`), not emoji;
  an empty name clears the state.
- `Channel.setTaskReaction` is a new vtable hook so the daemon can name a
  lifecycle state on any channel; the channel keeps ownership of the
  mapping from state to its own configured reaction. `Channel.TaskReaction`
  is shared with Telegram, whose existing enum becomes an alias.
- `SlackChannel.setTaskReaction` resolves the state to a plan of
  `reactions.add` / `reactions.remove` calls. Slack adds reactions instead
  of replacing them, so the state being left behind is removed explicitly;
  a state that reuses the name already on the message costs no call, which
  makes `accepted` and `running` sharing one name free.
- `daemon.processInboundMessage` emits accepted -> running -> done/failed,
  the same sequence the Telegram loop uses, including on every failure
  path so a turn never leaves a stale reaction behind.
- `already_reacted` and `no_reaction` are treated as success; an auth
  failure such as `missing_scope` disables reactions for the account after
  logging once.

The transition table is a pure function (`planTaskReaction`), so the state
machine is covered without a network.

Docs updated in docs/en/configuration.md and docs/zh/configuration.md.

Validation: `zig build test --summary all` (7589 passed, 14 skipped, 0
leaked) and `zig fmt --check` on the changed files, both in the Zig
0.16.0 container.
